+// Regression test for REVIEW-2026-11-07-22 B-01 (P1, carried from
+// REVIEW-2026-11-07-07): TLS unwrap conflated errors with clean EOF.
+// TlsRuntime.unwrap() returns null both when the peer cleanly closes
+// (Status.CLOSED) and when the SSLEngine throws (corrupt record, fatal
+// alert), and __tlsConnRead mapped every nil unwrap to io.EOF — so a
+// mid-connection TLS failure surfaced to Go code as a clean EOF instead
+// of an error (gc returns the alert/decrypt error). The fix adds the
+// graalgo.tls.unwrapError trap: the Java side records the exception, and
+// the Go side consults it on a nil unwrap.
+//
+// TestCorruptRecordReadReturnsError corrupts one byte of the first
+// post-handshake application-data record (via a corrupting net.Conn on
+// the server side, armed only after BOTH handshakes complete, so the
+// corruption can only hit application data, never a handshake flight).
+// Pre-fix the server's Read returns io.EOF; post-fix it returns a
+// non-EOF error containing "unwrap failed".
+//
+// TestCleanCloseReadReturnsEOF is the other half of the contract: a
+// genuine close_notify must STILL read as io.EOF (the fix must not turn
+// clean closes into errors).
+//
+// Cert fixtures copied verbatim from test/regression/tls-wrap-hang
+// (themselves from test/corpus/wave3/crypto/tls/01_handshake.go).
+//
+// No in-test timeout is possible (timers do not fire under graalgo,
+// PKG-048): a reintroduced hang blocks this test until test-regression's
+// per-directory `timeout 600` kills it and fails the gate, the
+// established pattern for hang regressions.
